\par
\b 1. Aim\b0\par
Implement a DevSecOps security pipeline using GitHub Actions and automated security scanning tools for the TaskFlow cloud-native application.\par
\par
\b 2. DevSecOps Concept\b0\par
DevSecOps integrates security into the development and delivery process. Security checks run automatically when code is pushed or a pull request is opened. This detects problems before code is merged or deployed.\par
\par
\b 3. Security Checks\b0\par
\tab Gitleaks scans the repository for accidentally committed secrets.\par
\tab npm audit checks Node.js dependencies for known vulnerabilities.\par
\tab CodeQL analyzes JavaScript source code for security problems.\par
\tab Trivy scans Kubernetes manifests for unsafe configuration.\par
\tab Trivy scans built Docker images for operating-system and package vulnerabilities.\par
\par
\b 4. Required Setup\b0\par
The project is already pushed to GitHub at the repository configured in Git. Docker Hub credentials were added as GitHub repository secrets:\par
\f1 DOCKERHUB_USERNAME\par
DOCKERHUB_TOKEN\par
\f0\par
The security workflow does not print or expose either secret.\par
\par
\b 5. Workflow File\b0\par
The workflow file is:\par
\f1 .github/workflows/security.yml\par
\f0\par
The workflow starts for:\par
\f1 push to main\par
pull request to main\nmanual workflow_dispatch\par
\f0\par
\b 6. Security Pipeline Jobs\b0\par
The workflow contains these jobs:\par
\tab Secret scan\par
\tab Dependency audit for frontend and all backend services\par
\tab CodeQL JavaScript analysis\par
\tab Kubernetes manifest scan\par
\tab Docker image scan for frontend and all backend services\par
\par
\b 7. Local Git Verification\b0\par
From the project root:\par
\f1 git status\par
git branch --show-current\par
git remote -v\par
git diff --check\par
\f0\par
Expected branch: \f1 main\f0 .\par
\par
\b 8. Publish the Workflow\b0\par
The workflow file must be committed and pushed to GitHub:\par
\f1 git add .github/workflows/security.yml\par
git add TaskFlow_DevSecOps_Security_Practical.rtf\par
git commit -m "Add DevSecOps security workflow"\par
git push origin main\par
\f0\par
\b 9. Run the Workflow\b0\par
Open the GitHub repository and select:\par
\f1 Actions -> DevSecOps Security\par
\f0\par
The workflow can run automatically after the push. It can also be started manually using:\par
\f1 Actions -> DevSecOps Security -> Run workflow\par
\f0\par
Take Screenshot 1: GitHub Actions workflow run.\par
\par
\b 10. Gitleaks Verification\b0\par
Open the \f1 Secret scan\f0 job. A successful result means no credentials were detected in the repository history scanned by the workflow.\par
Take Screenshot 2: Successful Gitleaks job.\par
\par
\b 11. Dependency Audit Verification\b0\par
The matrix checks these package directories:\par
\f1 frontend\par
services/user-service\par
services/task-service\par
services/stats-service\par
\f0\par
Each job runs:\par
\f1 npm install --ignore-scripts\par
npm audit --audit-level=high\par
\f0\par
Audit JSON reports are uploaded as GitHub Actions artifacts. High-severity findings fail the job.\par
Take Screenshot 3: Dependency audit jobs and uploaded report.\par
\par
\b 12. CodeQL Verification\b0\par
CodeQL analyzes JavaScript source files and publishes findings in GitHub Code scanning.\par
Open:\par
\f1 Repository -> Security -> Code scanning\par
\f0\par
Take Screenshot 4: CodeQL result.\par
\par
\b 13. Kubernetes Scan Verification\b0\par
Trivy scans the \f1 k8s\f0 directory for HIGH and CRITICAL configuration problems.\par
The scan checks Kubernetes manifests such as Deployments, Services, Secrets, ConfigMaps, and observability resources.\par
Take Screenshot 5: Successful Kubernetes Trivy scan.\par
\par
\b 14. Docker Image Scan Verification\b0\par
The workflow builds each Docker image in GitHub Actions and scans it with Trivy. Images scanned:\par
\f1 taskflow-frontend\par
taskflow-user-service\par
taskflow-task-service\par
taskflow-stats-service\par
\f0\par
The security workflow scans images but does not push them to Docker Hub. Image publishing belongs to the later CI/CD practical.\par
Take Screenshot 6: Successful Docker Trivy scan jobs.\par
\par
\b 15. Failure Behavior\b0\par
The workflow should fail when a high-severity dependency, image, or Kubernetes finding is detected. This prevents insecure changes from being accepted silently.\par
\par
\b 16. Troubleshooting\b0\par
\tab If the workflow is not visible, confirm the YAML file is pushed under \f1 .github/workflows\f0 .\par
\tab If Docker image scanning fails, inspect the Dockerfile and Trivy output.\par
\tab If npm audit fails, inspect the uploaded JSON artifact and update vulnerable dependencies.\par
\tab If CodeQL fails, open the job logs and GitHub Security tab.\par
\tab Never commit Docker Hub tokens, GitHub tokens, passwords, or Kubernetes credentials.\par
\par
\b 17. Result\b0\par
A GitHub Actions DevSecOps workflow was created for TaskFlow. It scans source code, secrets, dependencies, Kubernetes manifests, and Docker images before the application proceeds to the CI/CD stage.\par
\par
\b 18. Conclusion\b0\par
The security pipeline shifts security checks left into the development process. Automated scanning reduces the chance of secrets, vulnerable dependencies, insecure Kubernetes configuration, and vulnerable Docker images reaching production.\par
